: \"2026-01-15T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"expiresAt\": \"2026-01-16T08:00:00.000Z\",\n        \"statusUpdatedAt\": \"2026-01-15T08:00:00.000Z\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"budget\": {\n    \"searchesRemaining\": 9,\n    \"readsRemaining\": 40,\n    \"rawResultsRemaining\": 191\n  }\n}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_02_gA8Cjo7ZJV05Ahjk6eS94945"},{"content":"三个搜索都返回了同一批 3 个候选项，且证据完整度均为 partial、描述过于简略。缺失的内容细节（发布说明是否涉及类型系统、实践文章是否与演进相关、三者是否互为重复）足以改变准入决策，因此我读取这 3 个候选项的完整内容后再评估。","reasoning_content":"The three searches returned the same three candidates. All three candidates are pending admission:\n1. `candidate:2f581c6a-b092-4efd-8680-68b4e7e2b665` — \"TypeScript 发布说明\" (TypeScript Release Notes) — description: \"新版本发布说明。\" (Release notes for a new version)\n2. `candidate:49dbcce4-db07-4d82-8641-a4b713ab01ed` — \"TypeScript 编译器新进展\" (TypeScript Compiler New Developments) — \"类型推断与构建性能改进。\" (Type inference and build performance improvements)\n3. `candidate:fdae67e8-9519-4d28-9fef-b8d3c616b59f` — \"TypeScript 类型系统实践\" (TypeScript Type System Practice) — \"高级类型设计。\" (Advanced type design)\n\nAll three are from the same source (example.test), all have \"partial\" evidence completeness. They're cross-listed as potential semantic duplicates of each other.\n\nThe pool target is 6, low watermark 3. Currently 3 pending admission. We need to evaluate these.\n\nSince evidenceCompleteness is \"partial\" and the descriptions are thin, I should consider reading source candidates to get more detail. The instruction says: \"Call `read_source_candidate` only when missing detail can change the admission decision.\"\n\nLet me think about whether reading is needed. The candidates have thin descriptions:\n- \"TypeScript 发布说明\" — release notes page for a new version\n- \"TypeScript 编译器新进展\" — type inference and build performance improvements\n- \"TypeScript 类型系统实践\" — advanced type design\n\nThe interest is \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\" (TypeScript type system and compiler evolution). \n\n- Candidate 1 (release notes): release notes could be relevant if they describe new version features, but it's generic. Release notes for a new version would cover type system/compiler changes. But it's vague.\n- Candidate 2 (compiler new developments): directly relevant — type inference and build performance. Direct match.\n- Candidate 3 (type system practice): \"高级类型设计\" (advanced type design) — this is more of a practice/usage article about advanced type design, which may not be about type system evolution per se. But it could be relevant to the type system.\n\nThe potential duplicates: all three are semantically similar to each other per the system. But they are distinct URLs and distinct topics (release notes vs compiler progress vs type system practice). The system flagged them as potential semantic duplicates. Hmm.\n\nGiven the thin descriptions and partial evidence, reading the source candidates would give me the detail needed to make a proper admission decision. Missing detail (e.g., actual content) can definitely change admission decisions: whether release notes cover the type system, whether the type system practice is about evolution/compiler, and whether they're duplicates of each other.\n\nI have 40 reads remaining and only 3 candidates. Let me read all three to get full content. Actually, reads are somewhat expensive, but 40 reads remaining and budget is generous. Reading all 3 seems reasonable.\n\nLet me read all three candidates in parallel.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"candidateId\":\"candidate:2f581c6a-b092-4efd-8680-68b4e7e2b665\"}","name":"read_source_candidate"},"id":"call_00_uY48yNUFwblnWN62LcwS8640","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"candidateId\":\"candidate:49dbcce4-db07-4d82-8641-a4b713ab01ed\"}","name":"read_source_candidate"},"id":"call_01_CSkt8YsfnjnB2Bk51RJY1310","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"candidateId\":\"candidate:fdae67e8-9519-4d28-9fef-b8d3c616b59f\"}","name":"read_source_candidate"},"id":"call_02_KhJhwjD5orFOsfxCxAB42001","type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\n  \"code\": \"tool_execution_failed\",\n  \"message\": \"read_source_candidate failed\",\n  \"output\": {\n    \"status\": \"failed\",\n    \"code\": \"candidate_not_preparing\",\n    \"message\": \"Only a preparing Candidate can be read.\"\n  }\n}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_00_uY48yNUFwblnWN62LcwS8640"},{"content":"{\n  \"code\": \"tool_execution_failed\",\n  \"message\": \"read_source_candidate failed\",\n  \"output\": {\n    \"status\": \"failed\",\n    \"code\": \"candidate_not_preparing\",\n    \"message\": \"Only a preparing Candidate can be read.\"\n  }\n}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_01_CSkt8YsfnjnB2Bk51RJY1310"},{"content":"{\n  \"code\": \"tool_execution_failed\",\n  \"message\": \"read_source_candidate failed\",\n  \"output\": {\n    \"status\": \"failed\",\n    \"code\": \"candidate_not_preparing\",\n    \"message\": \"Only a preparing Candidate can be read.\"\n  }\n}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_02_KhJhwjD5orFOsfxCxAB42001"},{"content":"读取失败（候选项处于 `pending_admission` 而非 `preparing`，无法读取补充细节）。我将基于现有证据对这批 3 个候选项做评估：\n\n- **TypeScript 发布说明**：新版发布说明，直接覆盖类型系统与编译器新特性 → direct，substantive，admit。\n- **TypeScript 编译器新进展**：类型推断与构建性能改进 → 直接对应编译器演进 → direct，substantive，admit。\n- **TypeScript 类型系统实践**：高级类型设计，属于类型系统领域但偏实践用法，与「演进」主轴略有偏移 → adjacent，substantive，admit。\n\n三者内容形态（发布说明 / 编译器进展 / 类型实践）各不相同，非语义重复。无负面约束、无偏好方向。提交本批准入决策：","reasoning_content":"The reads failed because candidates are in `pending_admission` status, not `preparing`. So I cannot read them; the source detail is not available. I must evaluate based on the provided evidence.\n\nSo I evaluate the batch based on the available evidence:\n\nInterest: \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\" (TypeScript type system and compiler evolution). Active, no preference directions, no negative constraints.\n\nCandidate 1: `candidate:2f581c6a-b092-4efd-8680-68b4e7e2b665` — \"TypeScript 发布说明\" (TypeScript release notes) — \"新版本发布说明。\" (Release notes for new version.) Very thin. Release notes for a TypeScript new version would generally cover type system and compiler changes — that aligns with the interest \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\". Release notes are substantive (official release notes describing new features, type system changes, compiler improvements). Relevance: direct. Novelty: It's distinct from the other two (release notes vs compiler developments vs type practice)? The system flagged all three as potential semantic duplicates of each other. But the release notes page would be distinct content from the \"compiler new developments\" article. However, without being able to read, I must decide on the provided evidence.\n\nHmm, let me think about the semantics of the potential duplicate flags. The pending batch shows each candidate has the other two as potentialDuplicates with similarity \"semantic\". That could mean the system is suggesting they may overlap semantically. But titles and descriptions differ enough that they're plausibly distinct content:\n- Release notes (changelog of a version)\n- Compiler developments (type inference and build performance improvements) \n- Type system practice (advanced type design)\n\nThese are distinct. The \"semantic\" similarity flag is a signal to evaluate duplication. Given they describe different aspects (version changelog vs compiler progress vs type system usage), I'd consider them distinct and novel relative to each other.\n\nBut wait — should I admit all three? Let's check each:\n\n1. Release notes: content about a new TypeScript version release. This directly relates to type system & compiler evolution (new features in a release). Direct relevance, substantive (release notes detail new features). Temporal: valid (new version). Novelty: distinct from the other two (release notes are a changelog; not the same as \"compiler new developments\" article). Admit.\n\nActually, is release notes \"substantive value\"? Release notes do provide substantive value — they enumerate new type system features and compiler changes. Yes.\n\n2. Compiler new developments: \"类型推断与构建性能改进\" — type inference and build performance improvements. Directly about compiler evolution. Direct relevance, substantive, novel vs release notes and type practice. Admit.\n\n3. Type system practice: \"高级类型设计\" (advanced type design). This is about using the type system for advanced type design — a practice/usage article rather than about type system evolution/compiler. Hmm. The interest is \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\" — type system AND compiler evolution. An article about advanced type design is about the type system's capabilities — it could be adjacent. But is it about \"演进\" (evolution)? Not necessarily. It could be a tutorial about writing advanced types. That's more of a practice article. \n\nConsider whether the interest covers \"type system practice\". The interest says \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\" — the evolution of the type system and compiler. An advanced type design practice article is adjacent to the type system topic but isn't about evolution. However, \"类型系统\" as the interest domain includes the type system itself, and the description explicitly focuses on evolution. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider. The direction has no preference refinements, so the interest alone determines coverage. An article about advanced type design in TypeScript is about the type system — it matches \"TypeScript 类型系统\". The interest is \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\" which could be read as \"TypeScript type system and compiler evolution\" — i.e., both the type system and compiler, with a focus on evolution. An advanced type design article is relevant to the type system but not to evolution specifically.\n\nThe distinction: direct vs adjacent. For the type system practice article, I'd say adjacent — it's about the type system domain but not about the evolution/compiler evolution aspect. Adjacent relevance can still be admitted. Content value: \"高级类型设计\" — advanced type design is substantive (assuming a full article). \n\nBut we have no negative constraints, no preferences. The question is whether to admit all three.\n\nGiven the pool needs 6 candidates (target) with low watermark 3, and we currently have 3 candidates. If we admit all 3, we get 3 available. The target is 6. So after admitting these, we'd still need 3 more. But low watermark is 3 — pool target 6 with shortfall 6. Hmm, the gap shows totalShortfall 6. Interesting: the pool target is 6 and there are 0 available, so shortfall is 6. Admitting 3 would bring available to 3, still below target 6, and at the low watermark 3.\n\nSo I should admit these 3 and then search more to fill toward target 6.\n\nNow about the duplicate concern: since the three candidates are from the same source and cross-flagged as semantic duplicates, I need to decide whether they're truly duplicates or distinct. Given titles and descriptions:\n- \"TypeScript 发布说明\" / \"新版本发布说明\" — release notes page\n- \"TypeScript 编译器新进展\" / \"类型推断与构建性能改进\" — compiler progress article\n- \"TypeScript 类型系统实践\" / \"高级类型设计\" — type system practice article\n\nThese are distinct articles with distinct content. The semantic similarity flag may be due to all being about TypeScript. I'll treat them as distinct, novel candidates.\n\nHowever, I should be careful: the instruction says \"semantic novelty against only the supplied potential duplicates\". Each candidate's potential duplicates are the other two. Are they semantically duplicates? \"Release notes\" vs \"compiler developments\" vs \"type system practice\" — these are different content types. I'll admit all three as novel.\n\nFor each admission, I need to supply:\n- candidateId\n- reason\n- decision: admit\n- relevance: direct or adjacent\n- matchedInterestIds: [\"evaluation:interest:typescript\"]\n- contentValue: substantive\n- novelty: novel\n- temporalValidity: valid\n- negativeConstraint: clear\n- interestRevisions: [{\"interestId\": \"evaluation:interest:typescript\", \"revision\": 1}]\n- preferenceRevisions: [] — no preference directions; the exploration preference has revision 0 with no directions. Should I include preferenceRevisions? The schema requires interestRevisions and preferenceRevisions. The material says \"Submit the exact Interest and Preference revisions from Context plus only real Direction IDs through commit_candidate_admission.\" Context has interest revision 1, and preference revision 0 for the interest scope, and exploration preference revision 0. No actual directions exist. So preferenceRevisions should reference the scope keys with their revisions? The schema for admit requires \"interestRevisions\" and \"preferenceRevisions\" arrays of {scopeKey, revision} for preference, and {interestId, revision} for interest.\n\nHmm, the fields are `interestRevisions` (array of {interestId, revision}) and `preferenceRevisions` (array of {scopeKey, revision}). Since there are no preference directions, should preferenceRevisions be empty? The instruction says submit the exact Interest and Preference revisions from Context plus only real Direction IDs. The interest preference has scopeKey \"interest:evaluation:interest:typescript\", revision 0. Exploration preference has scopeKey \"exploration\", revision 0. \n\nI think we should include the interest revision (revision 1 for the interest) and include preference revisions for the interest scope and exploration scope at their current revisions (0), with preferenceAlignment referencing real Direction IDs — but there are none. Since no directions exist, preferenceAlignment is empty and preferenceRevisions can list the scope revisions. Actually the semantics: preferenceRevisions is probably used when the admission implies advancing a preference revision. Hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I'll include preferenceRevisions with the interest scope key at revision 0 (there are no direction changes) and exploration scope at revision 0. Actually, let me think — \"Submit the exact Interest and Preference revisions from Context\" — the Context provides the interest revision (1) and the preference revision (0 for the interest scope, 0 for exploration). So interestRevisions: [{interestId, revision:1}], preferenceRevisions: [{scopeKey: \"interest:evaluation:interest:typescript\", revision: 0}, {scopeKey: \"exploration\", revision: 0}]. And preferenceAlignment: [] since there are no real Direction IDs.\n\nWait, but \"only real Direction IDs\" applies to preferenceAlignment. Since there are no directions, preferenceAlignment should be empty.\n\nFor the type system practice article, relevance could be \"adjacent\" since it's about advanced type design (usage) rather than type system/compiler evolution. Hmm, but \"高级类型设计\" is a core type system topic. The interest name is \"TypeScript 类型系统与编译器演进\". \"类型系统\" (type system) is explicitly part of the interest. Advanced type design is about the type system. I could argue direct. But \"演进\" (evolution) suggests change over time, new features, roadmap. A practice article on advanced type design is more static usage. \n\nI'll consider: direct = the content is about the interest's core subject. The interest's core subject is the TypeScript type system and compiler evolution. An article on advanced type design is about the type system — arguably core. But it's about applying the type system (design patterns), not about the type system itself evolving. I'll mark it \"adjacent\" to be accurate: it relates to the type system domain but its focus (usage practice) is not the evolution focus of the interest.\n\nHmm, but the exploration_preference has no directions. There are no preference constraints. Being adjacent still allows admission. Actually, wait — should I even admit the practice article? Its value to the interest \"type system & compiler evolution\" is moderate. But no negative constraint blocks it. Adjacent content is acceptable to admit. I'll admit as adjacent.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The three cand